---
import Docs from "../layouts/Docs.astro";
import DocSection from "../components/docs/DocSection.astro";
import Callout from "../components/docs/Callout.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/nathansso/agentic_resume_tailoring";

// One list drives both the table of contents and the section headings.
const S = {
  short: { id: "short-version", title: "The short version" },
  passes: { id: "what-passes-through", title: "What passes through the lookup service" },
  kept: { id: "what-is-kept", title: "What is kept, and for how long" },
  others: { id: "other-parties", title: "Who else sees it" },
  own: { id: "your-own-profile", title: "Fetch your own profile only" },
  export: { id: "linkedin-export", title: "The LinkedIn data export instead" },
  limits: { id: "limits", title: "Limits" },
  contact: { id: "questions", title: "Questions" },
};
const toc = Object.values(S);
---

<Docs
  title="Privacy: ARTie"
  description="What the optional LinkedIn lookup sends, what ARTie keeps (quota counters only), and the LinkedIn data export you can use instead."
  heading="Privacy"
  lede="ARTie keeps your resume data on your computer. This page covers the one part that doesn't: the optional LinkedIn lookup."
  toc={toc}
>
  <DocSection section={S.short}>
    <p>
      ARTie runs on your computer, and your resume, your knowledge graph and your tailored
      versions stay there. The one exception is the LinkedIn lookup, which you can skip. When you
      give ARTie your LinkedIn URL, a small service run by ARTie asks a data provider for that
      profile and passes it straight back to you. <strong>The service keeps counters, not
      profiles.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>Last updated October 2026.</p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.passes}>
    <p>Each lookup sends three things through the service, and the service handles them only for as long as the request lasts:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your LinkedIn profile URL.</strong> Only addresses of the form
        <code>linkedin.com/in/your-name</code> are accepted. Anything else is refused.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A GitHub token</strong> from <code>art github login</code>. The service sends it
        to GitHub to learn your numeric GitHub user id, so it knows whose daily limit to use. It
        reads nothing else from your GitHub account, and it does not store the token.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The profile</strong> the data provider returns. The service hands it back to you
        and doesn't keep a copy.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The service never writes the URL, the token or the profile to its logs, and it adds no
      analytics or tracking. It also sees the network address your request comes from, which it
      uses for one thing: slowing down a single address that sends too many requests.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.kept}>
    <p>
      Counters are all the service keeps. They are stored under keys that carry no profile text, and each one expires on its own:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A daily count per GitHub user id.</strong> It holds a number and the date, and
        expires about two days after its last use.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A rate-limit count per network address, stored as a one-way hash</strong> rather
        than the address. It expires after two minutes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A monthly count of lookups across everyone</strong>, which is how the service
        stops before its budget runs out. It carries no user id and expires after about six weeks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A five-minute memory of a token check</strong>, stored under a hash of the token
        and holding your GitHub user id, so a burst of requests doesn't ask GitHub every time.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The service keeps no account for you, no profile data, no URLs and no tokens after the
      response is sent. Deleting the counters needs nothing from you: they expire by themselves.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.others}>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>GitHub</strong> receives your token to confirm who you are, the same as any app
        you sign in to with GitHub.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bright Data</strong>, the data provider, receives the LinkedIn URL and returns the
        profile. Its own terms and privacy notice apply to what it does on its side.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Vercel</strong> hosts the service and keeps its standard request records for
        a short time (the path, the status and the time of each request). ARTie adds no request
        bodies to them.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.own}>
    <Callout title="Please look up only your own profile" tone="warn">
      <p>
        LinkedIn's terms of service prohibit scraping, and the legal standing of the data
        provider's collection is unsettled. ARTie offers the lookup so you can bring your own
        profile in without downloading anything. Use it for your own profile, and no one else's.
        If you would rather stay clear of that question, use the data export below.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.export}>
    <p>
      The LinkedIn data export is always an alternative, and ARTie supports it fully. Request
      the archive from LinkedIn's own settings, then give your agent the file when you run
      setup. That route doesn't touch the lookup service, and it needs no GitHub login.
      <a href="/usage#ingest">The usage page</a> shows how.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.limits}>
    <p>
      The lookup is small by design. Each GitHub user gets a few profiles per day, one network
      address can only send so many requests a minute, and the service switches itself off for
      the rest of the month when its budget is used up. When any of these stops a request, ARTie
      says so and points you to the export.
    </p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection section={S.contact}>
    <p>
      To ask about data the service holds or to report a problem, open an issue in
      <a href={REPO} rel="noopener">the project's repository</a>.
    </p>
  </DocSection>
</Docs>
